# Argon2 hashes for the seeded DB accounts, fed ONLY to the postgres service
# (deploy/docker-compose.yml `env_file`, format: raw). Kept in this dedicated
# file — not the repo-root .env — so postgres never sees app-wide secrets
# (JWT keys, provider keys, MinIO creds) it has no use for.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH -> `admin`; DEV_PASSWORD_HASH -> `mobile` / `api` /
# `developer`. Applied by zz-seed-passwords.sh on a fresh volume and by
# scripts/migrations/2026-08-31-password-env.sql via apply_migrations.sh on
# existing ones (fail-closed there: an empty hash NULLs the account).
#
# Paste each hash UNQUOTED (format: raw keeps quotes literally, so 'HASH'
# would seed quotes into password_hash). Leaving them empty keeps the accounts
# password-less — /auth/login then returns 401 for them. Generate one:
#   python -c "from argon2 import PasswordHasher; print(PasswordHasher().hash('YOUR-PASSWORD'))"
#
# IMPORTANT for EXISTING stands: env_file values enter the container only at
# CREATION time. After adding this file, recreate the postgres service before
# running scripts/apply_migrations.sh — otherwise the applier (docker exec)
# sees empty hash variables, the fail-closed rotation migration NULLs all four
# seeded passwords and is still recorded as applied:
#   docker compose -f deploy/docker-compose.yml up -d postgres
#   scripts/apply_migrations.sh <postgres-container>
# (already ledgered with NULLs? re-run that file with --redo= after the
# recreation — see apply_migrations.sh.)
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
DEV_PASSWORD_HASH=
